WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:08.390
Foundations. The provider inside.

2
00:00:09.392 --> 00:00:11.922
Where does the Azure
code live in a service?

3
00:00:13.394 --> 00:00:15.004
Open the partition service.

4
00:00:15.394 --> 00:00:18.524
Its shared code is the
same in every cloud,

5
00:00:18.594 --> 00:00:21.164
and it calls a Service Provider Interface.

6
00:00:21.474 --> 00:00:24.764
The community has its own
implementation of that interface.

7
00:00:24.914 --> 00:00:29.644
The Azure implementation connects
the same interface to Azure services.

8
00:00:31.926 --> 00:00:35.696
The interface and its
implementation ship in one image.

9
00:00:35.846 --> 00:00:40.096
The build packs the shared code
and the Azure module into one JAR,

10
00:00:40.246 --> 00:00:42.496
and the image carries that JAR.

11
00:00:43.126 --> 00:00:47.296
So crossing the interface is a
method call inside one process.

12
00:00:47.366 --> 00:00:49.696
There is no network hop between them.

13
00:00:51.762 --> 00:00:54.802
The Azure implementation
lives in the service's fork,

14
00:00:54.802 --> 00:00:57.852
in its own provider folder
beside the shared code.

15
00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:03.200
Follow the same lookup
through the provider.

16
00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:06.710
The request reaches common
code, which calls the interface,

17
00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:08.160
with no network hop.

18
00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:13.120
The Azure implementation checks
its Redis cache inside AKS,

19
00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:18.150
and on a miss reads stored configuration
from Table Storage outside AKS,

20
00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:20.640
never the partition's own resources.

21
00:01:22.954 --> 00:01:24.244
So now you can say:

22
00:01:24.394 --> 00:01:27.514
the Azure code is a provider
behind an interface,

23
00:01:27.514 --> 00:01:30.244
shipped in the same image
as the code that calls it,

24
00:01:30.314 --> 00:01:32.324
and kept in the service's fork.

25
00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:38.340
Next: how does a change
reach a running service?

